# A trait's implementation is an override of the trait's method: `Store.get` runs the `get` of a type that implements
# `Store`, a choice between them, here `Shelf.get` alone, on the Store the caller calls it on (a value from beyond; the
# trait's declaration has no body to read it). `Shelf.get` builds a `Book` (a unit struct named as a value is a value of
# it) and hands it back at its own moment; the dispatch hands on what its override gave, at its own (law 4, Moment).

route books.Store
step Store()/Store.get
step Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get
step Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get/Book()

line entry > Store()/Store.get call
known #100 line Store > Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get value
line Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get/Book() > Book@Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get/Book() value
line Book@Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get/Book() > Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get value
line Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get > Book@Store()/Store.get/? value              # the override makes it at the dispatch's choice
line Book@Store()/Store.get/? > Store()/Store.get value
known #124 line Store()/Store.get > Book@Store()/Store.get value

home Store Store()
home Book@Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get/Book() Store()/Store.get/Shelf.get
home Book@Store()/Store.get/? Store()/Store.get
known #124 home Book@Store()/Store.get Store()                    # the dispatch hands on no Book: its signature names Self::Item

side entry left

# the override takes a value of its own class from beyond, and the Store ends at the border of the dispatch
